  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Isolated shower and thunderstorm chances continue\nthrough this evening. Potential for strong storms with heavy \nrain Friday and Saturday.\n\nThe large scale pattern continues to remain largely unchanged\nfrom earlier in the week, with upper level ridging remaining\nquasi-stationary to our southwest and upper trough over the\nnortheast with clusters of storms with embedded vorts track from\nthe Midwest into the mid-Atlantic. Upper flow and strength of\nthe clusters will dictate where they go, with our next potential\narriving late tonight into Friday morning, most models track \nthe system along/north of I-64, but some southeast trends into \nour area are possible. Timing roughly toward dawn in WV moving \neast into the morning. Going to keep pops highest in our WV \ncounties then tapering to low chance in the NC Piedmont. \n\nDepending on any sunshine Friday which seems less to the north\nand more over NC dictates severe threat. Still think a few\nstorms will bring gusty to potentially damaging winds across the\narea. \n\nAt the surface, a stationary front will generally remain within\nthe vicinity of the Virginia and North Carolina border through \nat least the weekend. For Saturday, similar setup with trying to\ntime systems upstream and impacts downstream over us in terms of\ntiming/cloud cover and impact for severe weather \n\nNot much change in the forecast though looks less active Sunday\nwith ridge building a little more, but then ridge gets flattened\nnext week with more shortwaves moving across, though flow is \nmore westerly which may keep storm coverage for east of the \nmountains somewhat limited.\n\nTemperatures through the period will likely stay above normal \nexcept a brief drop to near normal this weekend.",
